{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-48596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-48596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-48596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae9324723d527f13bc485752839a0bfe5eb9ac09a7230fc56893eab3402c0dc6"} {"question" "q-48596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-48596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=0$"] ["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-48596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ and $x=2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-99050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-99050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-99050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae9324723d527f13bc485752839a0bfe5eb9ac09a7230fc56893eab3402c0dc6"} {"question" "q-99050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-99050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=-3$ only"] ["text" "$x=-4$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-99050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-2$ and $x=2$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-127390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-127390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-127390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae9324723d527f13bc485752839a0bfe5eb9ac09a7230fc56893eab3402c0dc6"} {"question" "q-127390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-127390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-127390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-132464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-132464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+6-2{x}^{2}+4x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{10} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-132464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae9324723d527f13bc485752839a0bfe5eb9ac09a7230fc56893eab3402c0dc6"} {"question" "q-132464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-132464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-2\\pm \\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "x=-2\\pm \\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-132464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-345505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **approximate values**, rounded to **two decimal places**, of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-345505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-6)+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈-1.41$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x≈1.74$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-1.41,\\,x≈1.74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.41$ and $x≈1.74.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-1.41\\,\\text{and}\\,x=1.74\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-345505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae9324723d527f13bc485752839a0bfe5eb9ac09a7230fc56893eab3402c0dc6"} {"question" "q-345505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-345505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.41"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-345505" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988179/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f42ffcad38da9aebce422cf5b5a5a34ead1fae0800dfd99c1eebb3eaa24a2f5"} {"question" "q-345505" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.74"] "categ